   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to reporting of youth\nplaced in foster care settings and recruitment of foster parents; and to amend\na chapter of the laws of 2023 amending the social services law relating to\nreporting of youth placed in foster care settings and recruitment of foster\nparents, as proposed in legislative bills numbers S. 5897 and A. 2176, in\nrelation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8084",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8084-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §17, Soc Serv L; amd §3, Chap of 2023 (as proposed in S.5897 & A.2176)",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8084\n\nSPONSOR: BRISPORT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to reporting of\nyouth placed in foster care settings and recruitment of foster parents;\nand to amend a chapter of the laws of 2023 amending the social services\nlaw relating to reporting of youth placed in foster care settings and\nrecruitment of foster parents, as proposed in legislative bills numbers\nS. 5897 and A. 2176, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2023,\nc.739, to clarify provisions relating to the reporting requirements of\nyouth placed in foster care settings.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter by requiring the Office\nof Children and Family Services, by December 31, 2023, and biannually\nthereafter, to make information regarding youth placed in foster care\nsettings and the recruitment of foster parents publicly available on its\nwebsite, as opposed to providing a report to the Governor, the Senate,\nand the Assembly.\n\nThis legislation amends information that must be included in the report,\nexcluding therapeutic foster care from inclusion in foster care\nsettings, and requiring the following information:\n\n(i) Total number of youth placed in a foster care setting (at the time\nof the current report and a comparison from that of the previous\nreport);\n\n(ii) Where practicable reasons why a youth has been placed in a foster\ncare setting, which must include the total number of youths in foster\ncare at time of the report (reasons of which may include voluntary\nplacement);\n\n(iii) Where information is available the total number of youth placed in\na foster care setting that are in need or are receiving specialized\neducational services;\n\n(iv) Where information is applicable, how many youth were previously\nplaced in a foster care setting in New York state;\n\n(v) The total number of foster care families that are currently certi-\nfied in New York State (at the time of the report and whether the number\nhas decreased or increased since the previous report);\n\n(vi) Where information is available, the number of children and families\nwho've received preventative services, through a local social services\ndistrict, and of these children or families which subsequently entered\nfoster care within the last calendar year of the initial report and\nwhich were of these children or families since the previous report were\nsubmitted for subsequent reports;\n\n\n(vii) The total number of placement options that have certified as a\nqualified residential treatment program (as defined under Social\nServices Law section 409-h); and\n\n(viii) A description of efforts New York state has taken to assist local\nsocial service districts and voluntary agencies in the recruitment and\nretention of foster care parents within the state.\n\nLastly, this legislation amends the effective date by providing a date\nin which the section will expire and be deemed repealed, December 31,\n2025.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNone.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying chapter.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 739 of the Laws of 2023 S.5897 and A.2176\nboth passed the Senate and Assembly, respectfully, in June 2023 and May\n2023\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, that section\none of this act shall take effect on the same date in the same manner as\nthe chapter of laws of 2023, amending the social services law relating\nto reporting of 'youth placed in foster care settings and recruitment of\nfoster parents, as proposed in legislative bills numbers, 5.5897 and\nA.2176, takes effect.\n",
